2.13.20.m:
The Kṛttikās are the lunar mansion;
Agni is the deity.
You are the splendors of Agni,
of Prajāpati, of Soma, of Dhātṛ.
For verse, you!
For splendor, you!
For radiance, you!
For light, you!
With that meter, with that sacred word, with that deity, sit firm in the manner of Aṅgiras!
Rohiṇī is the lunar mansion;
Prajāpati is the deity.
Invagā is the lunar mansion;
the Maruts are the deity.
Bāhu is the lunar mansion;
Rudra is the deity.
Punarvasu is the lunar mansion;
Aditi is the deity.
Tiṣya is the lunar mansion;
Bṛhaspati is the deity.
Āśleṣā is the lunar mansion;
the serpents are the deity.
Maghā is the lunar mansion;
the Fathers are the deity.
The Phalgunīs are the lunar mansion;
Bhaga is the deity.
The Phalgunīs are the lunar mansion;
Aryaman is the deity.
Hasta is the lunar mansion;
Savitṛ is the deity.
Citrā is the lunar mansion;
Tvaṣṭṛ is the deity.
Niṣṭya is the lunar mansion;
Vāyu is the deity.
Viśākha is the lunar mansion;
Indra and Agni are the deity.
Anūrādhā is the lunar mansion;
Mitra is the deity.
Jyeṣṭhā is the lunar mansion;
Varuṇa is the deity.
Mūla is the lunar mansion;
Nirṛti is the deity.
Aṣāḍhā is the lunar mansion;
the Waters are the deity.
Aṣāḍhā is the lunar mansion;
the All-Gods are the deity.
Abhijit is the lunar mansion;
Brahmā is the deity.
Śroṇā is the lunar mansion;
Viṣṇu is the deity.
Śraviṣṭhā is the lunar mansion;
the Vasus are the deity.
Śatabhiṣaj is the lunar mansion;
Indra is the deity.
Proṣṭhapadā is the lunar mansion;
Ahi Budhnya is the deity.
Proṣṭhapadā is the lunar mansion;
Aja Ekapād is the deity.
Revatī is the lunar mansion;
Pūṣan is the deity.
The Aśvayujau are the lunar mansion;
the Aśvins are the deity.
The Bharaṇīs are the lunar mansion;
Yama is the deity.
Brāhmaṇa is the lunar mansion;
Soma is the deity.
You are the splendors of Agni,
of Prajāpati, of Soma, of Dhātṛ.
For verse, you!
For splendor, you!
For radiance, you!
For light, you!
With that meter, with that sacred word, with that deity, sit firm in the manner of Aṅgiras!

2.13.21.m:
Samīcī by name are you,
the eastern quarter;
of you, Agni is the overlord;
Asita is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Ojasyā by name are you,
the southern quarter;
of you, Indra is the overlord;
Tiraścīnarāji is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Prācī by name are you,
the western quarter;
of you, Soma is the overlord;
Svaja is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Suṣadā by name are you,
the northern quarter;
of you, Varuṇa is the overlord;
Sṛdāgu is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Avasthā by name are you,
the downward quarter;
of you, Viṣṇu is the overlord;
Kalmāṣagrīva is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Adhipatnī by name are you,
the upward quarter;
of you, Bṛhaspati is the overlord;
Citra is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!

3.1.9.m:
He offers these six ādhīta-yajus formulas.
Six indeed are the seasons;
by the seasons he thus raises the earth's vigor.
He offers [them] separately,
for these vital breaths are of varied vigor,
for the maintaining of the vital breaths.
If he desires of one, 'May he be deaf,' he should offer all of them for him running [them] together at once.
He splits his vital breaths;
he becomes deaf.
Therefore a deaf person speaks with speech, but does not hear;
for his faculty goes along into speech.
The vital breaths indeed are these other meters;
speech is the anuṣṭubh.
In that he offers the seventh with an anuṣṭubh,
he thereby connects speech with the vital breaths;
therefore this speech is the seventh of the vital breaths.
'Do not break easily, do not be easily injured,' with this he sets [it] down.
This was an Asura's magic;
the gods took it away with this yajus formula: 'An Asuric magic made by svadhā were you.'
Thereby the sacrificer takes away the magic of his rival.
With two [formulas] he sets [it] down;
two-footed is the sacrificer,
for firm standing.
He should set [it] down for one desiring prosperity;
we live upon that which is to come;
he indeed approaches that which is to come;
day after day he becomes better.
One whose fortune has arrived should put in an existing [fire];
he whose fortune has arrived is indeed an existing one;
by means of the existing he generates him.
He should bring [it] from a baker's oven for one whom he desires, 'May he be an eater of food.'
This indeed among fires is the eater of food;
the baker's oven is an instrument of food;
he secures proper food for him.
He should bring [it] from a forest fire for one whom he desires, 'May his realm be victorious with an army of conquest.'
His realm becomes victorious with an army of conquest.
Having fetched [it] from anywhere, he should put [it] in for one whom he desires, 'May an evil rival be born as his second.'
This indeed is the sacrificer's abode;
in his own abode he thus generates an evil rival as his second.
'Having wood as food, having ghee as oblation,' with this he puts on a krumuka-stick anointed with ghee.
This indeed is Agni's dear body, namely the krumuka;
ghee is fiery brilliance.
He provides him fully with his dear body and with fiery brilliance.
'From the farther region,' [with this] an udumbara [stick].
Where the gods divided nourishment, from there the udumbara arose.
In that it is of udumbara,
he thus secures nourishment.
'From the highest distance,' [with this] a vaikaṅkata [stick].
When Agni was created, his brightness departed;
it entered the vikaṅkata.
In that it is of vaikaṅkata,
he thus secures brightness.
'Whatever [wood], O Agni,' [with this] one made of śamī,
for pacification.
Indeed, nothing not cut with an axe was savory to Agni;
for him Asanga Prāyogi made everything savory with this [verse].
In that [he says], 'Whatever [wood], O Agni,'
thereby he makes everything savory for Agni;
everything becomes savory for him.
'Bearing [it] night by night without neglect,' with this
he invokes a blessing.
'Kindling Agni on the navel of the earth,' with this
he makes him a conqueror in battle.
When Agni was created, the rakṣases wished to slay [him];
he drove them away with these very [verses]:
'Which armies, approaching,' with this;
thereby he drives away the rakṣases from Agni.
He should put on these very firewood sticks when he fears rakṣases,
for the driving away of the rakṣases.
Moreover, in that season they seize thieves.
Practicing sorcery, he should put on a tilvaka-stick.
This indeed is the vajra among trees;
he dies suddenly.
Whom he hates, him he should then meditate upon with his mind;
the mind indeed is the blessing.
He shuts him off as one having become an offering of speech to Agni.
'Whoever plots against us, and which person hates us,' with this.
Therefore of an agnicit nothing ugly should be spoken,
nor of one who knows Agni.
'Sharpened is my sacred power, sharpened is my vigor and strength,' with this;
by the sacred power he thus sharpens the ruling power,
by the ruling power the sacred power; moreover, he makes sacred power and ruling power yoked together.
With reference to this, this [verse] has been recited:
'Sacred power and ruling power, yoked together, do not falter;
the sacred power indeed quickens the ruling power of the kṣatriya,
the ruling power quickens the sacred power of the brāhmaṇa, when they combine their vigors.'

3.2.2.m:
Now, this is the Vātsapra [hymn].
By this, indeed, Vatsaprī Bhālandana attained the dear domain of Agni.
Through this, he attains the dear domain of Agni.
What is dear comes to him.
Vatsaprī Bhālandana, indeed, the lying ones accused:
'They are thieves.'
He saw this hymn;
with it he conquered the accusation,
with it he attained honor.
Thus, with this he conquers accusation;
he attains honor.
He worships with twelve [verses].
Twelve months are a year;
having reached the year, he secures it.
They are triṣṭubhs,
for the securing of bodily power.
On alternate days he causes him to stride, on alternate days he worships.
He thereby maintains the acquisition and preservation of offspring;
therefore some offspring wander, others are settled.
Then, in that after striding he worships,
therefore the wanderer sets out against the settled one;
therefore the settled one became food for the wanderer.
On whichever day he is about to pile [the fire], on that day he should combine both:
he should stride forward and worship.
Hereby the sacrificer deprives his rival of acquisition and preservation, of food.
'Up may all the gods, O Agni,' [he says];
for all the gods are we here, namely humans.
'Bear thee with thoughts,' [he says];
for whichever thought the fire is set up, with that thought he enriches it.
'Go forward, O Agni, radiant,' [he says];
having enriched him with radiance, he causes him to blow forth.
'Agni has roared like thundering heaven,' thus he should recite if the axle should creak.
This [creaking axle] is capable, when creaking, of creaking out the offspring and cattle of the sacrificer;
he makes this [noise] a mere roaring for it;
then, having pacified it, he prays for the blessing.
'Honor Agni with fuel'—with a gāyatrī he should set it up for a brahmin;
for a brahmin is of gāyatrī nature;
with a triṣṭubh for a noble;
for a noble is of triṣṭubh nature;
with two gāyatrīs for a vaiśya;
for what are two gāyatrīs, that is a jagatī;
moreover, he thereby makes the common people food for the priesthood.
If the ashes should fill up, he should cause them to enter into the waters;
for waters are the womb of Agni;
he places it in its own womb.
He should make loose earth for one desiring cattle;
cattle, indeed, are loose earth;
he becomes possessed of cattle.
Or he should mix bricks;
thereby all his bricks become connected with Agni.
With vigor, indeed, with cattle he, departing, departs along [with it].
'Return again with vigor,' [he says];
thereby he secures vigor again, [and] cattle.
'Be aware of this speech of mine, youngest one,' [saying this] he worships with two [verses] containing 'be aware';
therefore creatures, having slept, awaken again.
He worships with two [verses];
the sacrificer is two-footed,
for firm standing.

3.3.2.m:
Now, this is the meter-layer [chandaściti].
The meters, indeed, are the desirable good of the gods, [and] cattle.
By this, indeed, the sacrificer obtains the meters, the desirable good, cattle.
Yajñasena Caitra, indeed, piled this;
he prospered.
He prospers who piles this [layer].
He sets [the bricks] down in front with Gāyatrī [verses].
The Gāyatrī, indeed, is brilliance, spiritual luster.
He obtains brilliance, spiritual luster.
He places three [bricks].
For threefold is Agni.
He sets them down with [verses] containing the word mūrdhan [‘head’],
thinking: ‘May I be at the head.’
With Triṣṭubh [verses] on the south.
Vigor, indeed, and virility is the Triṣṭubh.
He obtains vigor and virility.
With Jagatī [verses] behind.
Cattle belong to the Jagatī;
he obtains cattle.
With Anuṣṭubh [verses] on the north.
The Anuṣṭubh, indeed, is all the meters;
all the meters, indeed, become piled for him.
Then the Paṅktis;
then the Bṛhatīs;
then the Uṣṇihs;
then the Kakubhs;
then the Virājs.
Thus the meters become piled for him in regular succession.
He sets down two-footed [verses] as the last [bricks].
Two-footed is the sacrificer;
[it is] for firm standing.
The meters, indeed, are the desirable good of the gods, [and] cattle.
The Āhavanīya, indeed, is the sacrificer’s domain;
the dhiṣṇya hearths are the rival’s domain.
If he were to place [them] behind, he would place the desirable good, cattle, in the rival’s domain.
Now, because he places [them] in front,
he thereby places the desirable good, cattle, in the sacrificer’s domain.
He places eight [bricks].
Eight-syllabled is the Gāyatrī;
Agni belongs to the Gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so great he piles him.
He should place eight with Gāyatrī [verses] in front of the Bahiṣpavamāna if a rival should surpass him;
eleven with Triṣṭubh [verses] in front of the Mādhyaṃdina Pavamāna;
twelve with Jagatī [verses] in front of the Ārbhava Pavamāna.
He should place these same [bricks] behind the Pavamānas if a rival should surpass him.
The Pavamāna, indeed, is the out-breath;
the Pavamāna is the fiery brilliance of Agni.
With fiery brilliance, indeed, the sacrificer thereby repels the powerful rival;
with fiery brilliance he thrusts him back;
with fiery brilliance he drives him away.
That, indeed, is this driving away of the rival.
Piling [the altar] for the first time, he should pile it knee-high, piled with the Gāyatrī;
piling it for the second time, he should pile it navel-high, piled with the Triṣṭubh;
piling it for the third time, he should pile it chin-high, piled with the Jagatī.
If, having piled a larger [altar], he should pile a smaller one, he would approach a smaller sacrificial performance,
smaller offspring,
fewer cattle,
less food;
he would become worse off.
Now, because having piled a smaller one, he piles a larger one,
he approaches a greater sacrificial performance,
more abundant offspring,
more cattle, more food;
he becomes wealthier.
This, indeed, is Agni’s layer named ‘possessing higher stages’ [uttaravatī].
He becomes better and better, higher and higher tomorrow and tomorrow, who knows thus.

3.3.3.m:
It is to be made with five layers.
Fivefold is the sacrifice;
fivefold are cattle;
therefore it has five layers.
Five layers,
five [layers of] loose earth:
that amounts to the Virāj.
In that it amounts to the Virāj, therefore Agni’s praise is to be performed.
If it were to have five layers [without loose earth], it would fall away from the Virāj.
If it were to have ten layers, it would fall away from both the sacrifice and cattle.
Because it has five layers,
by that it does not fall away from the sacrifice and cattle.
Now, because there are five layers and five [layers of] loose earth,
by that it does not fall away from the Virāj.
By both these forms, indeed, he thereby obtains cattle for him:
that which of them is fivefold, and that which belongs to the Virāj.
Two-syllabled is hair [loman];
two-syllabled is skin [tvac];
two-syllabled is meat [māṃsa];
two-syllabled is bone [asthi];
two-syllabled is marrow [majjan];
that makes ten.
Ten-syllabled is the Virāj;
cattle belong to the Virāj;
he obtains cattle.
It is to be made with five layers.
These worlds, indeed, are three layers;
the sacrificer is two;
he has thereby established the sacrificer in these worlds.
‘What is the first layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
This [earth], indeed, is the first layer; plants are the loose earth.
‘What is the second layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
The intermediate space, indeed, is the second layer; birds are the loose earth.
‘What is the third layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
Yonder [sky], indeed, is the third layer; the constellations are the loose earth.
‘What is the fourth layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
The sacrifice, indeed, is the fourth layer; the sacrificial gift is the loose earth.
‘What is the fifth layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
The sacrificer, indeed, is the fifth layer; offspring are the loose earth.
So great, indeed, is [the universe];
as great as it is, so much he wins;
that he obtains.
Now, these are the season-bricks.
Agni Vaiśvānara, indeed, is the year;
this, indeed, is Agni’s dear body, namely Vaiśvānara;
the days and nights, indeed, are placed as his bricks.
He places [them] two by two,
for firm standing.
Having thrown [down] avakā plants, he sets [the brick] down,
for appeasement, for non-burning.
He places four [bricks] in the middle layer.
For the intermediate space is, as it were, unestablished;
[it is] for firm standing.
He places twelve [bricks].
Twelve months are the year;
Agni Vaiśvānara, indeed, is the year;
this, indeed, is Agni’s dear body, namely Vaiśvānara.
Agni is thereby piled in his dear body;
the sun is deposited possessing its body.
[With the formula:] ‘You are the inner clasp of Agni.’
Joined together, indeed, is the fire of him for whom these are placed;
joined together, indeed, is the fire of him for whom these are placed.
‘May heaven and earth be adjusted,’ [he says];
[everything] is adjusted, indeed, for him who has piled the fire;
he attains seniority before his equals, who knows thus.
He kindles Agni, indeed, when he piles [the altar];
he makes it blaze up with the season-bricks.

3.3.8.m:
'Lead him up higher,' [saying this] he puts on fire-sticks.
He has made a portion for him just as he is being carried forward.
He puts on three;
for Agni is threefold.
'Let all the gods up... you,' [he says];
for we who are mortals are all the gods.
'O Agni, let them bring with intentions,' [he says];
for whichever intention Agni is set up, he makes him succeed with that intention.
'May the five divine directions favor the sacrifice, the divine [directions],' [he says];
he makes him radiant in accordance with these five directions.
'Song-winged, worthy of praise, seized,' [he says];
for he has songs for wings.
'Having embraced the sacrifice, they have come,' [he says];
for they go having embraced him.
'Golden-haired, sun-rayed in front,' [he says];
that sun, indeed, is golden-haired, sun-rayed in front.
'Savitṛ, the light rising continuously,' he says
just for procreation.
'In his impulse wise Pūṣan moves,' [he says];
Pūṣan, indeed, is cattle;
he obtains cattle.
'Then may the recitations favor our prayers,' [he says];
this is the milking of wishes;
he milks wishes.
'All [songs] have magnified Indra,' [saying this] they go with [a verse] containing the word vṛdh ('to magnify');
they enter upon magnification.
They go with an Anuṣṭubh;
Anuṣṭubh is speech;
speech belongs to all the gods;
Agni belongs to all the gods;
therefore they go with an Anuṣṭubh.
They go with six [verses] from the Āgnīdhra;
six are the seasons;
they go with the seasons.
'This measurer sat in the middle of heaven,' [saying this] he sets down the stone;
for that sun is the measurer of the heavenly world.
'May the benevolence-invoking sacrifice bring [them] here, may god Agni sacrifice, and may he bring the god here,' [he says];
this is a prayer.
'May the god-invoking sacrifice bring [them] here, may god Agni sacrifice, and may he bring the god here,' [he says];
this is the clasp of the prayer.
'By the impulse of food, with the upward-grabber, he grabbed me upward,' [he says];
that sun is the upward-grabber;
this [fire] is the downward-grabber;
rising, he grabs the sacrificer upward;
setting, he grabs his rival downward.
'Both the upward-grabber and the downward-grabber,' [he says];
the upward-grabber is the sacred utterance;
the downward-grabber is the sacred utterance;
with the sacred utterance he grabs the sacrificer upward;
with the sacred utterance he grabs his rival downward.
'Now may Indra and Agni scatter my rivals on all sides,' [he says];
Indra and Agni are force and heroic vigor;
with force and heroic vigor the sacrificer thrusts away his rival on all sides.
They go with four [verses] to the Āhavanīya;
four are the meters;
they go with the meters;
further, the meters are the sacred utterance;
they go with the sacred utterance.

3.3.9.m:
'Stride up to the firmament with Agni,' he says,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
'Having gone to heaven, the back of the sky,' [he says];
for they go by the back.
'Bestow vigor upon our two-footed and four-footed,' [he says];
he places vigor in two-footed and four-footed cattle.
'Desiring to sacrifice together with the Bhṛgus,' [he says];
for the Bhṛgus initially prospered through sacrifice,
for prosperity.
'From the earth I ascended into the atmosphere,
from the atmosphere I ascended to the sky,' [he says];
this is the ascent of these worlds.
He steps up with five [verses];
the sacrifice is fivefold;
by means of the sacrifice he ascends the sacrifice;
[it is] for non-repulsion.
He offers with milk of a black [cow] having a white calf;
the calf is the day;
the mother is the night;
he makes night give [it] up to the day;
both day and night are milked for him who knows thus.
All cattle, being of various forms, have the same form in milk alone;
because they have the same form [in it], thereby he obtains them.
'Night and dawn,' [he says],
'O thousand-eyed Agni,
you are the fair-winged Garutmat;
sit on the back of the earth!'
He deposits Agni luminous in these worlds;
further, for the maintenance of the vital breaths.
He sets it down with two [verses];
two-footed is the sacrificer;
for stability.
'Offered to, fair of face in front,' he says,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
'That wondrous [glory] of Savitṛ the desirable,' [he says];
this milking of Agni, Kaṇva Śrāvayasa knew;
this is Agni's milking;
by this the fire-altar builder milks Agni;
Agni milks all milkings, all desires for him who knows thus.
'May we honor you in the highest birth, O Agni,' he says,
just for prosperity.
'Kindled forward, shine before us, O Agni,' [he says];
this is the pipe with ears, continuous light;
continuous light he has placed in him.
Vāsiṣṭha Sātyahavya asked those sitting at the sacrificial session:
'Do you know the pipe with ears?'
'We know,' they said, 'we know the one that is in the forests.'
Cattle are its ears;
he becomes possessed of cattle.
The gods rolled this thunderbolt, the hundred-slayer, the destroyer, against the Asuras;
they crushed them with a hundredfold blow;
the sacrificer rolls this very thunderbolt, the hundred-slayer, the destroyer, against his rival;
he crushes [him] with a hundredfold blow.
'O Agni, him today,' [saying this] he offers with a Paṅkti [verse];
the sacrificer is a Paṅkti;
he establishes the sacrificer in the Virāj;
further, he lays hold of the sacrifice with a Paṅkti oblation.
'Seven are your fire-sticks, O Agni, seven your tongues,' [he says];
so many are the bodies of Agni, sixfold, seven and seven;
whoever, having built the fire-altar, makes these of his dry up, dries up;
he satisfies these [bodies] of his.
'I offer the pile with my mind,' [he says];
this is Agni's undeceptive Vaiśvakarmaṇa oblation;
his rival does not deceive him;
he who has built the fire-altar attains preeminence among his equals, who knows thus.
Agni thought:
'Without a share, I will not hold this splendor,'
he held it with this share;
this Agnihotra is Agni;
with this the fire-altar builder holds Agni;
all milkings, all desires it milks for him who knows thus.
When he piles, he kindles Agni;
Agni inflames him with the Agnihotra.

3.3.10.m:
Now there is this twelve-pot-sherd [cake] for Agni Vaiśvānara.
Agni Vaiśvānara is the year;
he pleases the year.
Moreover, he has made a portion for him just as he has been carried forward.
By the firm establishment of the oblations the sacrificer is firmly established.
By the non-establishment of the oblations he is not firmly established.
When he offers this,
[it is] for the firm establishment of the oblations.
Following them as they are firmly established, the sacrifice is firmly established;
following the sacrifice, the sacrificer [is firmly established].
Moreover, Vaiśvānara is desire;
whatever desire he has, that desire yields to him.
If it were to turn east, the divine subjects would be bewildered.
If south, it would belong to Yama as deity.
If west, it would belong to Sūrya.
If north, it would belong to Rudra.
It is to be offered straight forward,
for firm establishment.
He makes it an entirely offered [cake],
for firm establishment.
Vaiśvānara is the royal power,
the Maruts are the commonalty.
When, having offered this, he offers those to the Maruts,
he thereby binds the commonalty to the royal power.
Moreover, he makes the commonalty obedient to the royal power.
Prajāpati created cattle with Agni as the mouth;
cattle belong to the Maruts.
When, having offered this, he offers those to the Maruts,
Prajāpati generates cattle for him with Agni as the mouth.
They are [cakes] on seven pot-sherds;
seven by seven are the troops of the Maruts,
therefore they are on seven pot-sherds.
He offers troop by troop;
he pleases the Maruts troop by troop.
The troop that is to be recited in the forest, that he should offer in the middle;
this [troop] is the royal power
of the Maruts, the others are the commonalty.
He thereby makes the royal power central to the commonalty.
If he should desire: 'May I slay the royal power with the commonalty,' he should confuse the troop that is to be recited in the forest with the other troops;
he thereby slays the royal power with the commonalty.
If he should desire: 'May I slay the commonalty with the royal power,' he should confuse the other troops with the troop that is to be recited in the forest;
he thereby slays the commonalty with the royal power.
Its offering-verse and invitatory verse should be Triṣṭubhs;
the Triṣṭubh is force and virility.
Having taken away force and virility from the commonalty, he thereby bestows them upon the royal power.
If he should desire: 'May I slay his royal power with royal power, may he fall from his own place,' he should dismember the carriage-pole facing the fire with that troop that is to be recited in the forest;
the carriage-pole facing the fire is the royal power among the Maruts;
it bears the royal power of the kṣatriya;
with royal power he slays his royal power;
he falls from his own place.
'Followers along Indra's path, the divine subjects, the Maruts,' [he says];
this is the following path of the gods;
he makes both the divine subjects and the human [subjects] his followers.
He kindles Agni when he piles him;
he makes him blaze up with the [oblations] to the Maruts.
// Thus ends the third prapāṭhaka, named Stomabhāga, in the upper kāṇḍa.

3.4.1.m:
He offers the shower of wealth,
for the formation of what is unformed, for the appeasement of what is unappeased, for the conquest of what is unconquered, for the containment of what is uncontained.
He offers continuously,
for the continuity of the vital breaths, for the continuity of these worlds, for the continuity of food.
He offers uninterruptedly,
for the non-interruption of food.
If he were to interrupt, he would interrupt the vital breaths.
If there is one whom he hates, he should interrupt for him;
he interrupts his food.
'Hungerlessness and food,' [he says];
these are the forms of food;
by means of forms he secures food.
'Fire and water,' [he says];
this is the womb of food;
he secures food along with its womb.
'May there be a shower of wealth for me,' [saying this] he offers the shower of wealth;
this is a shower of ghee;
[the drops] attend upon the sacrificer in yonder world.
He offers twelve by twelve;
twelve months are the year;
he secures the year.
He offers [oblations] half of which belong to Indra;
with the [oblations] half of which belonged to Indra the gods stripped off the Indra-power and virility of the Asuras;
with them the sacrificer strips off the Indra-power and virility of his rival.
The gods and the Asuras strove together.
Those gods said to Indra:
'With you as our leader let us conquer them.'
He said:
'Let there be a portion for me.'
'Choose!'
they said.
He said:
'May I have half-share of the deities.'
Thence they conquered;
therefore he has a half-share of the deities.
Since [oblations] half of which belong to Indra are offered,
[it is] for victory.
They have Indra at the end;
Indra is Indra-power and virility;
thereby the sacrificer is firmly established in Indra-power and virility, in the sacrifice.
'The Aṃśu and the Raśmi,' [he says];
the Aṃśu and the Raśmi are the face of the sacrifice;
he secures the face of the sacrifice.
'The Adhipati and the Adābhya,' [he says];
the rival does not harm him who has piled the fire-altar;
he attains seniority among his equals, who knows thus.
These are cup-drawings;
these are the forms of the sacrifice;
by means of forms he secures the sacrifice.
'The ladles and the cups,' [saying this] he gathers the sacrificial implements;
having gathered the sacrificial implements, he employs the sacrifice.
He offers vessels;
food is eaten with vessels;
according to its form he secures food.
'The sacrificial grass and the altar,' [he says];
this is a blessing.
'The concluding bath and the making of svagā,' [he says],
for firm establishment.

3.4.2.m:
'Agni and the heat-vessel,' [he says];
this indeed is Agni's holy luster;
by this he secures both radiance and holy luster;
he becomes possessed of splendor and possessed of holy luster.
'Season and observance,' [he says];
season and observance are day and night;
day and night become desired and pleased for him by this.
'One and three,' [he says];
one and three are the meters of the gods;
he secures the meters of the gods.
'Four and eight,' [he says];
four and eight are the meters of men;
he secures the meters of men.
He prospers in the world of the gods by the meter of the gods,
in the world of men by the meter of men.
'One and three, and four and eight,' [he says];
this is the climbing of these worlds,
the stepping over to the heavenly world.
By the chant increasing by two, the Ādityas went to the heavenly world;
by that increasing by four, the two Aṅgirases [went].
When he offers these two chants,
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
These two, even and odd, are a pair
for procreation.
He places seed by the one increasing by two;
he brings forth the placed seed by the one increasing by four.
'One and three,' [he says],
up to thirty-three: thirty-three are the deities;
they become desired and pleased for him by this.
'Four and eight,' [he says],
up to forty-eight.
The Jagatī has forty-eight syllables;
cattle belong to the Jagatī;
he secures cattle.
'The three-year-old steer and the three-year-old heifer,' [he says];
these are the ages of cattle;
by means of cattle he secures cattle.
'May life-span be formed through the sacrifice,
may breath be formed through the sacrifice,' he says;
this is the formation of the sacrifice;
he has formed the sacrifice in this way.
'To vigor, svāhā!
To impulse, svāhā!' [he says];
these are thirteen oblations;
the year has thirteen months;
he places the year for him in the heavenly world;
he is established in it.
'The chant and the sacrificial formula,' [he says];
the chant and the sacrificial formula are food;
having placed food in himself, having become an eater of food, he moves forth at the end to the domain of the gods.
Agni is the Vasu;
this is his stream;
for him Agni yields all milkings, all desires, who knows thus.
Agni kindles this food when he builds [the altar];
he illuminates it with the shower of wealth.

3.4.4.m:
He offers the vājaprasavya;
by this Agni won vigor, food;
by this the sacrificer wins vigor, food.
He offers for food after food with the vājaprasavya formulas;
vigor is food,
for securing food.
He offers both cultivated and wild,
for securing both kinds of food.
He offers with a ladle made of udumbara wood;
udumbara wood is nourishment,
for securing nourishment.
'At the impulse of the divine Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, with the voice of Sarasvatī, with the control of the Controller, I consecrate Bṛhaspati to imperial rule,' thus he should consecrate if a brahmin should sacrifice;
for this is the Bṛhaspatisava.
'I consecrate Indra to imperial rule,' thus he should consecrate if a nobleman should sacrifice;
for this is the Indrasava.
On a black antelope skin he should consecrate one desirous of holy luster;
this is the form of the Brahman, of the Ṛc and Sāman;
in the Ṛc and Sāman is holy luster;
he secures holy luster.
On a goat skin [he should consecrate] one desirous of cattle;
a goat skin is cattle;
he secures cattle.
He among the fires is consecrated who is piled up;
with his own consecration he causes him to prosper.
The gods ran a race when the plants ripened;
Agni won them;
this is the consecration of Agni;
he is consecrated for food.
This is one of the consecrations;
this is the Bṛhaspatisava;
a brahmin belongs to Bṛhaspati by deity;
he is consecrated with his own consecration.
Concerning this they say:
'It must indeed be offered; for one who has consecrated himself descends to no one.'
He is consecrated with a deity who is a fire-altar builder.
The seasons uphold the realm for one who has consecrated himself;
the seasons are six;
the seasons are the realm-sustainers;
when he offers with six [oblations],
he establishes his realm in the seasons;
they uphold the realm for him.
He offers with [a ladle] taken twelve times;
the year has twelve months;
having reached the year, he secures it.
These oblations are paired:
'To him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!'
In that pair he places seed.
He offers the seventh on the chariot head,
for the conquest of the directions;
conquered by the chariot, the directions are won.
Now these are the offerings to the wind;
for the fire-altar builder are day and night yonder;
for the fire-altar builder this wind blows;
wind is breath;
he secures breath.
He offers with joined palms;
for there is no cutting off of this;
moreover, [it is] for enclosing.
Wind is breath;
the hand is most suited for breath;
therefore he offers with joined palms.
He offers three times;
three are these worlds;
these worlds blow the wind for him.
'A sea art thou, cloudy, dripping moisture,' [he says];
these are the forms of the wind;
by its forms he secures the wind.
'Pertaining to the Maruts art thou, the troop of the Maruts,' [he says];
for the wind pertains to the Maruts.
Agni kindles this when he builds [the altar];
he illuminates it with the offerings to the wind.

The gāyatrī is the out-breath;
the cart-shed belongs to the Gāyatrī.
The jagatī is the down-breath;
the sadas belongs to the Jagatī.
The triṣṭubh is the through-breath;
the Āgnīdhra belongs to the Triṣṭubh.
The life-spirit is speckled.
'Speckled stone placed in the middle of heaven,' [he says];
he places this between his out-breath and down-breath in the middle,
for the retention of the vital breaths.
He places [the stones] with the stone as the ninth in the Āgnīdhra,
for nine are the vital breaths;
he thus places the vital breaths in his sacrificer-world.
Twenty-one in the Hotṛ's [hearth],
for firm standing;
for the twenty-first is a firm standing.
Eleven in the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin's [hearth];
the triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
the triṣṭubh is heroic power;
he stands firmly in heroic power.
Eight each in the others;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the gāyatrī is the Brahman;
he stands firmly in the Brahman.
Six in the Mārjālīya;
six are the seasons;
the seasons carried him around from the south;
the seasons are the Fathers.
Moreover, six are the meters;
he thus yokes the meters at the opening of the sacrifice.
'United, the year with the Āyus,' [he says];
these are Agni's deities having shares in front;
he gratifies them.
Moreover, here are the dear bodies of the gods;
he secures them.
'United, the year with the Āyus,' [he says];
the year is the abda;
the seasons are the Āyus;
by this the year and the seasons become desired and pleased for him.
'United, Dawn with the ruddy cows,' [he says];
he gratifies Dawn.
'United, the two Aśvins with wonder-works,' [he says];
the two Aśvins are day and night;
day and night become desired and pleased for him by this.
'United, Sūrya with Etaśa,' [he says];
he gratifies Sūrya.
'United, Vaiśvānara with Iḍā, with clarified butter, svāhā,' [he says];
Vaiśvānara is the year;
Iḍā is cattle;
clarified butter is cattle;
by this the year and cattle become desired and pleased for him.
He offers on a clump of darbha-grass;
this is his immortal-piled body;
thus Agni is piled in what is immortal.
Having placed gold, he offers;
he offers on what has fire, what has an abode;
blind would the Adhvaryu become if he were to offer on what has no abode.
For the heavenly world the chariot of the gods is yoked; the chariot of men for desire.
'I yoke Agni with strength, with clarified butter,' [he says];
he thus yokes Agni;
with him yoked he goes to the heavenly world.
If he were to yoke with all [verses], his sacrifice would be yoked, [but] the oblations would be without firm standing.
He does not yoke with two,
for the firm standing of the oblations.
When they stand firmly, the sacrifice stands firmly after [them];
the sacrificer stands firmly after the sacrifice. He yokes with three;
for threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, so great he yokes him;
when he is yoked, the whole oblational offering is placed.
He yokes with five;
the sacrifice is fivefold;
as great as the sacrifice is, so great he seizes it.
These two indeed scorch him: he who is piled and he who is Vaiśvānara.
When he touches with [two verses] containing 'waters' before the Yajñāyajñīya [sāman],
[it is] for appeasement.
This is the measure of the sacrifice, namely the Agniṣṭoma;
but what is done higher from this is an increase for him.

3.4.5.m:
Whoever does not yoke Agni when the yoking has arrived does not yoke among those who yoke.
'O Agni, yoke those that are yours,' [he says];
he thus yokes Agni when the yoking has arrived, he yokes among those who yoke.
Whoever does not unyoke Agni when the unyoking has arrived does not unyoke among those who unyoke.
'I untie your halter, your reins,' [he says];
he thus unyokes Agni when the unyoking has arrived;
he unyokes among those who unyoke.
Just as the re-establishment [of the fires], so is the re-piling.
He who prospers by the establishment establishes [them] again.
He who, having piled Agni, should think, 'May this prosper for me,' he should pile this re-piling.
Even he who piles for the first time may pile [it],
for prosperity;
he indeed prospers;
moreover, for pairing.
Whatever oblation the fire-altar builder offers without a brick, it flows away;
flowing away, the sacrifice flows after it;
the sacrificer flows after the sacrifice.
When he places this re-piling,
[it is] for the firm standing of the oblations.
When they stand firmly, the sacrifice stands firmly after [them];
the sacrificer stands firmly after the sacrifice.
He places eight;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
Agni belongs to the Gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so great he piles him.
Having placed eight space-fillers, he covers them with loose earth;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the gāyatrī is the Brahman;
with the gāyatrī meter, with the Brahman, he piles him.
Having placed eleven space-fillers, he covers them with loose earth;
the triṣṭubh has eleven syllables; it is heroic power;
with the triṣṭubh meter, with heroic power, he piles him.
Having placed twelve space-fillers, he covers them with loose earth;
the jagatī has twelve syllables;
cattle belong to the Jagatī;
with the jagatī meter, with cattle, he piles him.
This is Agni's high altar;
thus Agni is piled possessing a high altar.
Whatever fire was piled for him previously, having settled down beside it he should sacrifice.
Just as here one puts more [fuel] on what is burning, so indeed he successively places more fiery splendor in this [altar].
Concerning this they say:
'Who will awaken the superior when he is sound asleep?'
When he sacrifices, he deprives him of the oblation.
He who piles the fire makes a home;
whatever one gains from elsewhere, one brings it to the home.
He who sacrifices either with an iṣṭi, or an animal sacrifice, or with Soma, whatever fire was piled for him previously, having settled down beside it he should sacrifice;
just as here, having acquired [goods] elsewhere, one brings them to the house, so is that.
Concerning this they say:
'Again indeed is this done with the ladle in the sacrifice.'
Wherever he sacrifices, having placed these twenty-one [bricks] he should sacrifice, for firm standing;
for the twenty-first is a firm standing.
